{
  "requirement": "Inspection preserves the requested path scope and the selected language for\noutlining and structural search.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The subject has two obligations: inspection preserves the requested path scope, and it preserves the selected language for outlining and structural search. The reviewed tests cover both.\n\n**Path scope**\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources/tests.rs#fn[defaults_use_provider_primitives_and_keep_one_parse_snapshot]` runs `search_text` with prefix `src` against the `Memory` provider.\n  - `Memory::list` ignores its scope argument and also returns `other/two.txt`.\n  - Every `read` returns text containing \"hit\". The test still asserts exactly 2 matches and an event log in which only `src/one.txt` is read. So the out-of-scope resource is excluded.\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources/tests.rs#fn[structural_search_uses_overridden_parses_their_snapshot_and_requested_scope]` runs structural search with prefix `src` against `CachedParse`.\n  - `CachedParse` lists `src/one.rs` and `other/two.rs`, and both would match `call()`.\n  - The test asserts a single match at path `src/one.rs` with selector `fn[cached]`, and a parse count of 3 over its three runs, one parse each. So the out-of-scope file is neither parsed nor matched.\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/source_defaults.rs#fn[gitignore_is_shared_by_all_operations_with_or_without_a_git_repository]` covers the real local provider.\n  - It checks that scoped `list` and `search_text` behave correctly for file prefixes, directory prefixes, a normalized prefix (`./src/../src`), and an escaping prefix (`..`, which must error).\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/source_defaults.rs#fn[scoped_inspection_skips_unrelated_directories_and_reports_relevant_ignore_errors]` shows that a scoped `list` of `src` returns only `src/one.txt`, and does not fail on the broken ignore file in an unrelated directory.\n\n**Selected language**\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources/tests.rs#fn[list_read_only_provider_supports_selected_language_parse_and_structural_search]` checks parsing and structural search with the language the provider selects.\n  - The provider's explicit language `rust` parses a resource named `generated` and yields `fn[provided]`.\n  - The explicit language wins over the `.py` extension.\n  - With no explicit language, the `.rs` extension selects Rust.\n  - A structural query for `rust` finds `call()` with the right path and selector.\n  - A query for `python` against a Rust-language source is complete and empty. An unknown language is an error.\n  - A malformed rule is an error, and a parse that returns a different resource is an error.\n\n**Other obligations**\n- The `parse_snapshot` and `search_completeness` child requirements are separate requirements with their own tests, so they are not assessed here.\n- I found no plausible violation that these tests would accept.\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/source_defaults.rs#fn[built_in_languages_and_structural_search_use_the_project_scope]` also exercises the local provider's structural search. It has no `{test}` reference to the subject, but the tests above already cover the obligation, so this is not a gap.",
  "references": "fb25fa326e584dedcc46b4cb5ffb943c498c41361dc844a88aafec157fd70a3f",
